#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# Requirements: flock
#
# Author: James Cherti
# URL: https://github.com/jamescherti/bash-stdops
#
# Description:
# ------------
# Run a command at most once per specified time interval (in seconds).
#
# Usage: run-every <seconds> <timestamp_file> <command...>
#
# Examples:
#   # Run a backup script at most once per hour (3600 seconds):
#   run-every 3600 ~/.cache/backup.last-run /usr/local/bin/backup
#
#   # Update dotfiles in ~/.bashrc at most once per day (86400 seconds):
#   run-every 86400 ~/.cache/dotfiles-sync.last-run git -C ~/dotfiles pull -q
#
# This is a one-shot wrapper, not a background daemon or scheduler.
#
# How it works:
# - First run: If <timestamp_file> does not exist, it creates the file (and any
#   missing parent directories) and runs the command immediately.
# - Subsequent runs: Checks the modification time of <timestamp_file>. If fewer
#   than <seconds> have passed since the last run, it prints "[IGNORED]" and
#   exits with status 0.
# - State tracking: The timestamp is updated right before the command starts,
#   meaning failed runs still reset the cooldown timer. When the command runs,
#   its exit status is passed through.
# - Concurrency: Uses <timestamp_file>.lock to prevent overlapping runs. If
#   another instance is still running, new invocations print "[IGNORED]" and
#   exit with status 0.
